# Round XI Track M — KNOWN GAP (D36 unimplemented + `.get()` return-type
# propagation, HIGH).
#
# Class 3 of 4 filed by Track M: CHAINED `m.lock().get().greet()` on a
# `Mutex[Speaker]` (bare trait). Distinct from class 1 in the intermediate
# `.get()` hop — the checker accepts the whole chain, but `Guard[Speaker]
# .get()` mistypes its return as the raw inner slot (`int64_t` placeholder
# for the trait-object), so downstream dispatch fabricates the symbol
# `int64_t__greet` which resolves at LINK TIME (not C-emit as in class 1,
# nor at check as in class 2/2b) — the ld error `undefined reference to
# 'int64_t__greet'`.
#
# Under D36 (ratified 2026-07-27, `docs/define-gorget/decisions.md:1363-1381`)
# `Guard[T].get()` should propagate T faithfully AND the method call on the
# returned value should auto-deref through the wrapper per D36 Q1 (read
# face accepts uniformly for Guard/Read/Write/Box). Two layered defects
# roll together here — the `.get()` return-type propagation gap is worth
# calling out separately because it is the fabricator: with the correct
# T-typed `.get()` return, dispatch would either find `Speaker::greet`
# or fall back to the D36 auto-deref path.
#
# DISTINCT FROM Track J (`sound_guard_get_string_double_free.gg` — heap-inner
# ownership on non-trait inners with a REGISTERED `.get()` returning the
# inner by-value; that's an OWNERSHIP CONTRACT bug on non-trait heap-inners,
# NOT a type-propagation/dispatch bug on trait-inners). Same `.get()` node,
# different defect roots.
#
# HEAD behavior (probe 2026-07-28):
#   gg check <this>            → OK: no semantic errors (exit 0)
#   gg build <this> -o /tmp/p  → ld error (printed to stderr, wrapper still
#     exits 0 — Core #15(d) probe note):
#     undefined reference to `int64_t__greet'
#     collect2: error: ld returned 1 exit status
#     C compiler exited with: exit status: 1
#     (link stage — distinct from class 1's C-emit stage; no binary produced)
#
# FIX DIRECTION (Option A / D36-implementation, DEFERRED to Round XII+):
# extend `Guard[T].get()` return-type propagation to preserve trait inners
# (defect 2 and 3 are `.get()`-return-type issues; defect 1 is the D36
# vtable-dispatch gap on the wrapper itself).
#
# INTENDED (after Option A): prints `beep R2`.

trait Speaker:
    String greet(self)

struct Robot:
    String name

equip Robot with Speaker:
    String greet(self):
        return "beep " + self.name

void main():
    print(Mutex[Speaker](Robot("R2")).lock().get().greet())
